      "title": "Club Management in Iran: Ownership and Football Licensing",
      "description": "Understand documented Iranian football licensing decisions, ownership changes, and the distinction between organizational requirements and verified compliance."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "responsibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The organization behind the team",
        "content": "A football team’s results are only part of its eligibility for continental competition. The Asian Football Confederation, or AFC, defines the licence applicant as the legal entity responsible for the team. Its framework addresses organizational capacity and financial responsibility as well as sporting requirements. For readers encountering Iranian clubs through match coverage, this explains why administrative decisions can affect participation independently of performances on the field. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "licensing-case",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented licensing consequence",
        "content": "On 7 January 2022, the AFC announced that its independent Entry Control Body had withdrawn the licences of Persepolis, Esteghlal, and Gol Gohar Sirjan for the 2022 AFC Champions League. It stated that the clubs had not satisfied all mandatory criteria and should not have received licences. [s2]\n\nThe announcement establishes the decision and its stated regulatory basis. It does not identify each club’s individual deficiencies. Assigning the same specific explanation to all three clubs would therefore go beyond this source. The decision is also historical: it does not establish any club’s licensing position in a later season. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "ownership-case",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Ownership changes require separate scrutiny",
        "content": "A Persian-language report dated 3 Ordibehesht 1403, corresponding to 22 April 2024, described transactions involving controlling share blocks in Esteghlal and Persepolis. Citing Iranian media and Tasnim news agency, Iran International reported purchasers associated with petrochemical businesses for Esteghlal and a banking consortium for Persepolis. The report connected the transactions with an approaching professional-licensing deadline and concerns about common ownership. [s3]\n\nThis is evidence of reported transactions at that date. It is insufficient to determine the clubs’ ultimate beneficial ownership, subsequent board appointments, or present operating independence. Those questions require corporate filings and later licensing records. A transfer of shares alone does not demonstrate that financial controls, staffing, or creditor payments have improved. That distinction is an editorial inference about what the report can establish, rather than a finding about either club’s subsequent performance. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading requirements alongside outcomes",
        "content": "The AFC’s 2026 framework distinguishes mandatory criteria, criteria whose failure can attract sanctions while a licence remains possible, and recommendations. A reference to a licensing requirement therefore needs its applicable category and season. The framework also seeks timely settlement of liabilities and stronger management. These are regulatory objectives; evidence of compliance must come from decisions and club records. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
