        "id": "sponsorship",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What sponsorship seeks to achieve",
        "content": "Sponsorship brings resources to a sporting organization in exchange for commercial benefits. A study published in 2013 examined this relationship among supporters of Persepolis and Esteghlal, two Iranian football clubs. Its research question concerned how attachment to a team related to supporters’ perceptions of sponsors. This is a useful distinction: supporting a club and responding favorably to a business associated with it are related questions, but they are not identical. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "findings-and-meaning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different outcomes require different evidence",
        "content": "The researchers used a statistical model to examine relationships between team attachment and several marketing outcomes. They reported positive relationships with corporate image, purchase intention, and loyalty, but did not find the hypothesized positive relationship with brand awareness. [s1]\n\nThe finding cautions against treating sponsor recognition, favorable opinion, and intended purchasing as interchangeable measures. It also does not establish actual sales, a financial return on a sponsorship contract, or a causal effect applicable to every supporter. These are limits on interpretation: the study’s reported relationships should remain attached to its research setting and date. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "research-boundary",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From a historical study to a current account",
        "content": "This page currently supports a narrow conclusion about how sponsorship effectiveness has been studied in Iranian football. It cannot establish the size of today’s sponsorship market or the performance of a current campaign. A publication-ready account of contemporary practice still requires inspected contracts or disclosures, documented campaign activity, and evidence from sports and audiences beyond the two clubs studied.",
